  "recipeCuisine": "United Kingdom",
  "name": "Monkfish Osso Bucco with Gremolata",
  "text": "Monkfish Osso Bucco with Gremolata\n\nPreparation time:\n\nCooking time:\n\nYield: 4\n\nIngredients\n\n2 x 250g Monkfish tail (cut into steaks),\n\nplain flour (for dusting),\n\nsalt & pepper,\n\nextra virgin olive oil (for frying).\n\nFor the sauce\n\n60g unsalted butter,\n\n1/2 onion (peeled and finely chopped),\n\n1 garlic clove (peeled and crushed),\n\n1 small carrot (peeled and finely chopped),\n\n1 celery stick (finely chopped),\n\npinch of saffron,\n\n3 sprigs thyme (chopped),\n\n2 tbsp tomato puree,\n\n125 ml white wine,\n\n250 ml beef stock.\n\nFor the gremolata\n\n2 garlic cloves (peeled and crushed),\n\n1 lemon (zested and finely chopped),\n\n15g bunch parsley (finely chopped),\n\n3 tbsp olive oil,\n\nsalt & pepper.\n\nEquipment\n\nMedium sized heavy based saucepan,\n\nSmall glass bowl,\n\nMetal handled frying pan (suitable for the oven).\n\nPreparation\n\n1. Mix all the ingredients for the gremolade in a small glass bowl, season, then set aside.\n\n2. Pre-heat the oven to 200C/Gas6.\n\n3. Melt half the butter in a medium sized heavy based saucepan over a low to medium heat and cook the onion, garlic, carrot, and celery until soft, without colouring.\n\n4. Add the saffron, thyme, and tomato puree and cook for a minute.\n\n5. Stir in the white wine and reduce by three quarters.\n\n6. Add the beef stock, bring to the boil and simmer until reduced by half.\n\n7. Set aside until the monkfish is ready.\n\n8. Lightly flour and season the monkfish.\n\n9. Heat a nonstick frying pan until smoking, add the fish, and cook for 3 minutes on each side until golden brown.\n\n10. Transfer to an oven proof dish and place in the oven for 8 minutes.\n\n11. Reheat the sauce and whisk in the rest of the butter.\n\n12. Place the fish on plates, pour over the sauce, then drizzle with gremolata.",
  "recipeYield": "4",
